course: dictionary
lesson: 15076
title: Rigidly: stiff and strict
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Rigidly tells you how something is done. It comes from the word rigid, which means stiff and hard to bend. When you add -ly, it describes how an action happens. «The metal bar was fixed rigidly to the wall, so it could not move.» The bar did not wobble or bend, so rigidly must mean stiffly, firmly, and without any movement.
explain: Rigidly can also mean strictly, with no changes allowed. «The school rules are rigidly enforced.» The rules are followed exactly, and nobody is allowed to break them or bend them a little. Ask yourself: is something being held stiff, or being kept strict? The sentence will show you which one.
explain: A good check is to swap in a simpler word and see if the sentence still makes sense. «The soldier stood rigidly still when the captain walked past.» If you swap in stiffly, the sentence still works. Stiffly and strictly are the two main meanings to remember.
fact: Rigidly is an adverb, so it tells you how an action is done.
fact: Rigid is the root word, and it means stiff and not easy to bend.
fact: «Rigidly» can mean stiffly, in a way that does not bend or move.
fact: «Rigidly» can also mean strictly, without changing a rule at all.
fact: Rigidly and loosely are opposites: a rigidly fixed shelf stays still, while a loosely fixed shelf may wobble.
exercise: «The metal bar was rigidly fixed to the wall.» What does rigidly mean here? | *Held firmly so it cannot bend or move | Bent over and curved | Hanging loosely | Painted bright red
exercise: «The soldier stood rigidly still when the captain walked past.» How was the soldier standing? | Slouched and relaxed | Lying flat on the ground | *Stiff and still, not bending | Dancing around
exercise: «The school rules are rigidly enforced.» What does rigidly suggest? | Rules are sometimes forgotten | Rules are only for fun | Rules are written in pencil | *Rules are strictly followed, with no changes
test: Mum says the bedtime rule is applied rigidly every night. What does that suggest? | *It is always followed strictly, with no changes | It only applies on Sundays | Sometimes it is forgotten | It is just a joke
test: «She held her arm rigidly out straight.» Which word is closest in meaning to rigidly here? | Happily | Quickly | Gently | *Stiffly
exam: What is the best meaning of rigidly in «The frame must be fixed rigidly, so it cannot wobble»? | Happily | Loosely | *Firmly, in a way that cannot bend or wobble | Quickly
